        "result": "I fixed the bug in `median()` in `stats.py`, and all 3 tests now pass.\n\n**The bug:** `median()` always returned the single middle item after sorting. That's right for odd-length lists, but for even-length lists the median is the average of the two middle values. For example, `[4, 1, 3, 2]` sorts to `[1, 2, 3, 4]`, so the median should be `(2 + 3) / 2 = 2.5`, but the function returned `3`.\n\n**The fix:** For even-length lists, `median()` now returns the average of `ordered[middle - 1]` and `ordered[middle]`. Odd-length lists work as before.\n\nFollowing the project's Python style guide, I also added type hints and docstrings to `mean()` and `median()`.\n\n`median()` and `mean()` still fail on an empty list (`IndexError` and `ZeroDivisionError`). That was already true, and I didn't change it since the tests don't cover it.",
